                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_E(b,f) : F_E(b,a) \to F_E(b,a')
                  be the function that sends
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi e
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \stackrel{e}{\to} a
                  to the codomain of the unique lift of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi f
                        %mi a
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \stackrel{f}{\to} a'
                  with this domain and mapping to
                  = succeed ";." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Id
                            %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_{b}
            %p One checks that this yields an equivalence of categories.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_F
              is equivalently characterized as being the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
              of the diagram
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi F
                      %mo ←
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to K_F \leftarrow A
          %p
            Note that profunctors can also be characterized by their collages, these being the two-sided
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+cofibrations"} codiscrete cofibrations
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                cocomma object
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cocomma+object"} ?
          %h3#relation_to_distributive_laws Relation to distributive laws
          %p
            Fibrations and opfibrations on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (or more generally an object of a suitable 2-category) are the algebras for a pair of pseudomonads. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has pullbacks, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-distributive+law"} pseudo-distributive law
            between these pseudomonads, whose joint algebras are the two-sided fibrations satisfying the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#vonGlehn"} von Glehn (2015)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"}> Grothendieck fibration
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Street+fibration"}> Street fibration
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"}> discrete fibration
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+in+a+2-category"}> fibration in a 2-category
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %strong> two-sided fibration
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Conduche+functor"} Conduche functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
          %h2#references References
          %p An early reference is the notion of “regular span” on page 535 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nobuo+Yoneda"}> Nobuo Yoneda
              ,
              %em On ext and exact sequences
              (PhD thesis), Journal of the Faculty of Science, Section I.
              %strong 8
              University of Tokyo (1960) 507–576 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://alpha.math.uga.edu/~lorenz/YonedaExtExactSequences.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://ci.nii.ac.jp/naid/500000325773"} CiNii:naid/500000325773
          %p Original discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                \.
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Fibrations and Yoneda’s lemma in a 2-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Fibrations in bicategories
              %p 21(2):111–160, 1980. (Corrections in 28(1):53–56, 1987)
          %p Further discussion of discrete fibrations
          %ul
            %li#BournPenon
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominique+Bourn"}> Dominique Bourn
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Penon"} Jacques Penon
              = succeed "." do
                %em 2-catégories réductibles
              %em TAC Reprints
              no. 19, 2010 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/19/tr19abs.html"}> link
              ).
          %p Useful reviews are in
          %ul#Riehl
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
              ,
              %em Two-sided discrete fibrations in 2-categories and bicategories
              2010 (
              %a{:href => "https://math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/fibrations.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"} Fosco Loregian
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} Emily Riehl
              = succeed "," do
